    "justification": "The band supports simultaneous claims about the entire dynamic treatment-effect path without an unobservable active-coordinate set.",
    "gap": "The published Causal-MP confidence-interval discussion is heuristic, while generic Gaussian-max theory does not derive the common-disorder and nuisance influence terms.",
    "consumer": "The one-batch, zero-ridge \\(\\texttt{CFEEstimator}\\) can return analytic simultaneous path bands, including at zero-variance coordinates.",
    "proof_tex": "Apply the finite-recursion Hadamard expansion in \\(\\text{ass:path-smoothness}\\) to the joint parameter expansion in \\(\\text{thm:parameter-law}\\).  The common third-derivative envelope, compactness, and the common interior and rank margins make its remainder uniform.  Since the parameter and initial-law influence direction for row \\(\\xi_i\\) is exactly \\(\\zeta_T(\\xi_i)\\), linearity of the derivative gives\n\\[\n \\sqrt N(\\widehat\\theta_T-\\theta_T)\n ={1\\over\\sqrt N}\\sum_{i=1}^ND\\Psi_T(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2,\\tau^2,P_{0X})[\\zeta_T(\\xi_i)]+o_P(1)\n ={1\\over\\sqrt N}\\sum_{i=1}^N\\psi_{\\theta,T}(\\xi_i)+o_P(1).\n\\]\nThis proves the first display uniformly.  Applying the same derivative to the joint Gaussian limit gives a centered Gaussian vector.  Its covariance is\n\\[\n P_\\xi[\\psi_{\\theta,T}(\\xi)psi_{\\theta,T}(\\xi)^{\\top}]=\\Omega_\\theta\n\\]\nby definition, proving the stated limit law.\n\nWe next prove feasibility.  The influence-level cancellation established in \\(\\text{prop:residual-cancellation}\\) is\n\\[\n S_mI_T(\\xi)=R,\n \\qquad S_vI_T(\\xi)=R\\circ R-\\operatorname{diag}(C).\n\\]\nCentering \\(\\widehat e_i\\) removes the common fitted message-mean error, and the score Taylor expansion used in the parameter theorem therefore gives\n\\[\n {1\\over N}\\sum_i\\|\\widehat\\zeta_i-\\zeta_T(\\xi_i)\\|^2=o_P(1)\n\\]\nuniformly, where the norm on the last component is the Euclidean norm of its values on the finite class \\(\\mathcal F_{0X,T}\\).  Uniform laws of large numbers also give \\(\\widehat J\\to J\\), \\(\\widehat Q_\\beta\\to Q_\\beta\\), \\(\\widehat A_v\\to A_v\\), and \\(\\widehat L\\to L\\).  Continuity of the forward derivative of the finite recursion and the common envelope then imply\n\\[\n {1\\over N}\\sum_i\\|\\widehat\\psi_{\\theta,T,i}-\\psi_{\\theta,T}(\\xi_i)\\|^2=o_P(1).\n\\]\nCauchy--Schwarz and the uniform law of large numbers for \\(\\psi_{\\theta,T}\\psi_{\\theta,T}^{\\top}\\) now yield\n\\[\n \\widehat\\Omega_\\theta={1\\over N}\\sum_i\\widehat\\psi_{\\theta,T,i}\\widehat\\psi_{\\theta,T,i}^{\\top}\n \\longrightarrow\\Omega_\\theta\n\\]\nuniformly in probability.\n\nFor coverage, let \\(G_\\Sigma=\\max_t|Z_t|\\) for \\(Z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\Sigma)\\).  On a covariance family satisfying \\(\\inf_Q\\max_t[\\Omega_{\\theta,tt}]_Q>0\\), compactness and bounded second moments imply that the covariance matrices form a compact set excluding the zero matrix.  For every matrix in this set, \\(G_\\Sigma\\) has no atom: at least one Gaussian coordinate is nonconstant, and the event that a finite maximum of absolute Gaussian coordinates equals a prescribed positive number is contained in a finite union of zero-probability coordinate level sets; its value at zero also has probability zero.  If uniform continuity of these distribution functions at their \\((1-\\alpha)\\)-quantiles failed, a convergent covariance subsequence would contradict this atomlessness.  Thus covariance consistency implies uniform convergence of the conditional critical values in the quantile sense needed below.  Combining it with the uniform Gaussian approximation of the preceding asymptotic linear sum gives\n\\[\n \\liminf_N\\inf_QP_{N,Q}\\{\\max_t|\\sqrt N(\\widehat\\theta_{T,t}-\\theta_{T,t})|\\le c_{1-\\alpha}\\}\\ge1-\\alpha,\n\\]\nwhich is exactly simultaneous membership in \\(\\mathcal C_N\\).  At a fixed \\(Q\\) with a continuous nondegenerate maximum law, its quantile has probability exactly \\(1-\\alpha\\), so the limit is equality.\n\nThe additional covariance separation is necessary for the uniform coverage clause as formulated.  Here is an explicit local certificate.  In the scalar regular experiment \\(\\sqrt N(\\widehat b-b)=Z\\), with \\(Z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)\\), take the smooth target \\(\\Psi(b)=b^2\\), estimate its influence covariance by \\(4\\widehat b^2\\), and take \\(b=2/\\sqrt N\\).  If \\(q=z_{0.975}\\), multiplication of the coverage inequality by \\(N\\) shows that its limit is\n\\[\n \\mathbb P\\{|4Z+Z^2|\\le2q|2+Z|\\}\\approx0.8765<0.95.\n\\]\nThe four boundary points are the roots of \\(z(z+4)=\\pm2q(z+2)\\), so the displayed probability is directly computable from the standard normal distribution function.  This quadratic coordinate is embedded in the smooth affine family\n\\[\n g_\\beta(y,w)=y/2+w+\\beta+\\beta^2w:\n\\]\nthe additive term makes the mean Jacobian nonzero near \\(\\beta=0\\), the affine base retains the witness's variance excitation and positive Gram pivots, and the first counterfactual contrast is \\(1+\\beta^2\\).  Thus a compact local sequence approaching \\(\\beta=0\\) satisfies the regular model conditions while exhibiting the scalar mechanism.  Absolute covariance consistency and pointwise first-order expansions do not prevent this uniform undercoverage when \\(\\Omega_\\theta\\) collapses.",
